(
    version: 1,
    id: "m03",
    title: "Turn on the Spot",
    chapter: 1,
    concepts: ["Differential drive", "Wheel base"],
    world: (id: "open_grid"),
    robot: Starter("rover"),
    time_limit_s: 12.0,
    grading_seeds: [1, 2, 3],
    battery_soc: (0.7, 1.0),
    brief_md: r#"
## Spin without wandering

Turn the Rover **left by 90 degrees, then left by another 180 degrees** (270 degrees in all,
counter-clockwise) and stop. When you are done the heading must be right to within 3 degrees, and the
hub must not have drifted more than 20 mm from where it started.

A robot turns on the spot when one wheel goes forwards and the other goes backwards by the same
amount. How far must each wheel roll for a turn of 90 degrees? That depends on how far apart the
wheels are.
"#,
    predict: Some((
        question: "The left wheel turns forward once and the right wheel turns backward once. How many degrees does the Rover turn?",
        unit: "degrees",
        measured: "spin_one_turn_deg",
    )),
    criteria: [TurnTo(yaw: 4.71238898038469, tol_rad: 0.05235987755982989, drift_m: 0.02)],
    stars: (
        (text: "Heading within 1 degree", criteria: [TurnTo(yaw: 4.71238898038469, tol_rad: 0.017453292519943295, drift_m: 0.02)]),
        (text: "Drift under 5 mm", criteria: [TurnTo(yaw: 4.71238898038469, tol_rad: 0.017453292519943295, drift_m: 0.005)]),
    ),
    hints: (
        "Each wheel rolls along a circle of radius half the wheel base. For a turn of angle A (in radians) each wheel must roll (wheel base / 2) times A.",
        "Measure the wheel base: spin the Rover once with the motors set to opposite angles and see how far it turned, or read the distance between the wheel centres in the builder (143.8 mm).",
        "Left motor gets +a and right motor gets -a, where a = (wheel base / 2) * turn angle / wheel radius. Use axl_motor_position and wait until axl_motor_done is true before the next turn.",
    ),
)
